{"term":"kick off","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:6f9713128df614d49e","definition":"To make the first kick in a game or part of a game.","example":"The players kick off for the third quarter and the clock starts.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"ambitransitive","register":"ambitransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:4c0d9820821398b0d2","definition":"To start; to launch; to set in motion.","example":"Let's kick off this project with a planning meeting.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["start off"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"ambitransitive, idiomatic","register":"ambitransitive, idiomatic","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:bd9f772109061f1a85","definition":"To dismiss; to expel; to remove from a position.","example":"I got kicked off the team after a string of poor performances","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:a8782344b6366dc1eb","definition":"To die or quit permanently.","example":"It's a wonder that old dog hasn't kicked off yet.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"US, colloquial, euphemistic, idiomatic","register":"US, colloquial, euphemistic, idiomatic, intransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:b4183d805f616ab9a4","definition":"To shut down or turn off suddenly.","example":"The washer was working fine until it kicked off in the middle of a cycle.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"US, idiomatic","register":"US, idiomatic, intransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:73c7d4beecc15a2993","definition":"To suddenly become more active.","example":"The party kicked off when the third bottle of wine was opened.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"idiomatic","register":"idiomatic, intransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:d95e490c629bdbaf1d","definition":"To force the weaning of a bovine cow's calf by restricting the calf's access to its mother's udders, whether by literally kicking it away or another method.","example":"A week after we kicked off her calf that cow was still bawling.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"US, idiomatic, slang","register":"US, idiomatic, slang, transitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:6d574df3fefde4f27d","definition":"To be overcome with anger, to start an argument or a fight; to behave aggressively.","example":"When she called him a drunk, it was the last straw. He just kicked off.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"UK, colloquial, idiomatic","register":"UK, colloquial, idiomatic, intransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:f937189812a8dc7b38","definition":"To have a fight or argument start; to fight or argue.","example":"It really kicked off in town when the team lost.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"colloquial, idiomatic, impersonal","register":"colloquial, idiomatic, impersonal, intransitive","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/kick_off#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"}],"pronunciation":{"us":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/16/En-au-kick_off.ogg/En-au-kick_off.ogg.mp3"},"wordFamily":[]}